package Vixen.Shaders.Pipeline

import Vixen.Shaders.Core
import Vixen.Shaders.Shading
import Vixen.Shaders.Geometry
import Vixen.Shaders.Material

// The forward shading pass: written once against `IMaterialSurface`, instantiated per material.
//
// This is what the whole composition mechanism exists for. The shader knows nothing about metalness,
// glossiness, normal maps or emission — it fills a `MaterialData` from whatever feature the material
// chose, and shades it. Adding a material feature does not touch this file.
//
// "Forward *plus*" names the light culling: a compute pass bins lights into clusters so a fragment
// iterates only the lights that reach it instead of all of them. Both halves now exist —
// `ClusterCulling.rvn` is the binning pass — and `[Permutation] UseClusteredLights` chooses which
// loop this shader has:
//
//   off  a uniform array of `MaxLights`, filled by the host. Right for a scene with a handful of
//        lights, where a dispatch and a 450 KB buffer cost more than iterating all of them.
//   on   the cluster list, read from the buffer the culling pass wrote.
//
// It is a permutation rather than a branch because the two need different *code*: off, the clustered
// variant's `positionVS` stream folds away entirely, and with it the interpolator every fragment
// would otherwise pay for. A runtime branch would keep both loops in every variant.
//
// ⚠ It does **not** fold the bindings. A binding is in the plan because it is declared, not because
// a variant reads it, so a shader compiled with clustering on still declares the per-object light
// list and one with `UseImageBasedLighting` off still declares the environment cube — and a host
// still has to bind them, because a set is written wholly or not at all. Eliminating bindings no
// variant reads is a compiler feature Raven does not have; this comment claimed it did.

shader ForwardPlus {
    /// The material's surface features, chosen per material.
    compose val surface: IMaterialSurface

    /// What the surface does with a light, chosen per material.
    ///
    /// The second half of the same idea: `surface` decides what the point *is*, this decides how it
    /// answers a light. Two slots rather than one because the two vary independently — a clear coat
    /// over a metal-roughness base and over a specular-glossiness one is the same lobe — and folding
    /// them together would be one shader per pair.
    compose val shading: IShadingModel

    /// Whether to add image-based ambient. Off gives a pure punctual-lit look, and folds the IBL
    /// arithmetic out of the variant — though not its bindings, which are declared rather than
    /// discovered. See the note at the top of the file.
    [Permutation] val UseImageBasedLighting: bool = true

    /// Whether to sample a shadow map for the directional light.
    [Permutation] val UseShadows: bool = true

    /// Whether to read the culled cluster list instead of a host-filled uniform array.
    [Permutation] val UseClusteredLights: bool = false

    /// Whether this pass's materials are records of one buffer rather than a set bound per draw.
    ///
    /// What `docs/bindless-materials.md` is about: a draw that binds a descriptor set per material
    /// cannot be merged with a draw that binds a different one. On it, the whole per-material block
    /// below becomes one record of a buffer subscripted by `materialIndex`.
    ///
    /// ⚠ Off by default, and the default is the half that runs everywhere. Records need
    /// `HasBindless`, which GL, WebGL2 and MoltenVK below argument-buffer tier 2 do not have — so a
    /// host asks for this only where the device said yes, and gets the pass it always had otherwise.
    [Permutation] val UseMaterialRecords: bool = false

    /// Whether the object's world matrix comes from a buffer instead of from a push constant.
    ///
    /// The other half of the same argument. Bindless materials stop a draw needing a descriptor set of
    /// its own; this stops it needing a *push* of its own — and a push is per draw by definition, so a
    /// pass that keeps one can never merge two draws however little else they differ in. On it, the
    /// matrix is at the object's slot in `transforms` and the draw carries the slot in its own
    /// `firstInstance`, which the API adds into `SV_InstanceID` before this shader runs.
    ///
    /// ⚠ Off by default, and off is not degraded. A pushed `mat4` costs no memory read at all, so
    /// this is a straight loss on any frame that is not merging — which is every frame on a device
    /// without `HasDrawIndirectCount`, since without a device-side draw count there is no compacted
    /// list to merge over. `TransformRenderFeature.EnableRecords` gates on exactly that.
    [Permutation] val UseTransformRecords: bool = false

    /// How many entries the uniform light list has room for. Unused when clustering is on.
    ///
    /// A permutation rather than a constant because it is the *host's* budget: it sizes a uniform
    /// array, so it sets the descriptor's size, and a project that ships eight lights per draw should
    /// not pay for sixty-four. It is also why `lightCount` exists separately — the array is sized
    /// once at compile time and filled to a different depth every draw.
    [Permutation] val MaxLights: int = 16

    // --- Set 3: what varies per object ---------------------------------------
    //
    // Everything below used to be unmarked, which is set 2 — the material's. That was wrong in a way
    // that showed up as nothing: `ForwardLightingRenderFeature` writes exactly this block and binds
    // it at set 3, so the shader and the feature that fills it disagreed about which set it was in,
    // and neither said so. A set marker per binding is what makes the four-set convention a thing
    // the shader states rather than a thing a host remembers.

    /// Object to world, pushed rather than bound. Unused when `UseTransformRecords` is on.
    ///
    /// A push constant because that is what `TransformRenderFeature` already does with it, and the
    /// shader saying anything else would be the same silent disagreement this whole audit is about.
    /// It also leaves the per-draw block with exactly one owner — the lighting feature — where a
    /// block holding both a transform and a light list needs two features to agree on its layout.
    ///
    /// ⚠ Still declared with the records on, and the range is still in the pipeline layout. A binding
    /// is in the plan because it is declared and not because a variant reads it — the note at the top
    /// of this file — and a push constant is no different. Nothing pushes it in that variant, which
    /// costs the layout 64 of its guaranteed 128 bytes and costs a frame nothing.
    ///
    /// `worldViewProjection` is gone with it: it was world × the view's matrix, computed per object
    /// on the CPU and uploaded per object, where the vertex stage can multiply two matrices it
    /// already has. Sixty-four bytes less per object, and one less thing that can be a frame out of
    /// date with the camera it was built from.
    [PushConstant] var world: mat4

    /// Which record of the material buffer this draw's material is, when materials are records.
    ///
    /// **A push constant, and it was a per-object block member — which was the wrong shape.** A
    /// variant is keyed `(material, flags, shader)`, so one variant is one material; a batch is keyed
    /// on the variant, so every object in a merged draw has the *same* material and the same record.
    /// It is per *draw* and never per object, and putting it in the per-object block meant a
    /// clustered frame — which binds no per-draw set at all, because a fragment finds its own lights
    /// in the grid — never delivered it. Bindless materials and clustered lighting were quietly
    /// exclusive.
    ///
    /// It also takes a cross-feature write with it: the lighting feature used to write the material
    /// feature's number into its own block, which its own comment apologised for. And the block is 68
    /// bytes against Vulkan's guaranteed 128, so it fits everywhere `world` already did.
    ///
    /// ⚠ `[MaterialIndex("UseMaterialRecords")]` rather than `[MaterialIndex]`: the marker turns this
    /// shader's whole per-material block into one record of a buffer, and that is what a device with
    /// bindless wants and not what GL, WebGL2 or MoltenVK below argument-buffer tier 2 can do
    /// (ADR-011). Conditional on the permutation, one shader is both — and the *default is the
    /// fallback*, so a host that never asked gets exactly the pass it had.
    [PushConstant] [MaterialIndex("UseMaterialRecords")] var materialIndex: uint = 0u

    // The scalars come first and the array last, which is not a style choice: std140 starts an array
    // of structures on a sixteen-byte boundary whatever precedes it, so three scalars and four bytes
    // of padding fill exactly the header `ForwardLightingRenderFeature.HeaderSize` already writes.
    // Declaring the array first would put the count after 1280 bytes of lights, and the feature that
    // fills this block writes it at zero.

    [PerDraw] var lightCount: int = 0

    /// Which of `probes` lights this object.
    ///
    /// Half of what per-object reflection probes cost, and they are numbers rather than bindings
    /// because the cubes are one array bound for the frame. The alternative — a descriptor set per
    /// probe, bound per draw — is a set per object in all but name, which is the cost the four-set
    /// convention exists to refuse.
    [PerDraw] var probeIndex: int = 0

    /// How much of the probe shows through, against the environment behind it.
    ///
    /// What makes probes blend rather than pop: a surface deep inside a probe's volume takes it
    /// whole, and one near the edge fades back to the sky over the probe's falloff distance. Per
    /// object because that is what the falloff is a function of — where the object is.
    [PerDraw] var probeWeight: float = 0f

    /// The host-filled light list. Read `lightCount` entries, not `MaxLights` — the rest is stale
    /// memory. Unused when clustering is on.
    [PerDraw] var lights: PunctualLight[MaxLights]

    // --- Set 1: what varies per view -----------------------------------------
    //
    // The block every shader in the frame shares, and the reason it is stated here in the same terms
    // `ViewConstants` writes it: a descriptor set survives a pipeline change only if the layouts
    // agree up to that set, so what set 1 holds is a contract between shaders rather than any one
    // shader's business. Only a block belongs here — a texture or a buffer would make two shaders'
    // set 1 incompatible and the shared set unbindable.

    /// World to clip for this view.
    [PerView] var viewProjection: mat4

    /// Where the view is, which every specular term measures against.
    [PerView] var viewPosition: float3

    /// World to view, for placing a fragment in the cluster grid. Unused when clustering is off.
    [PerView] var view: mat4

    // --- Set 0: the scene ----------------------------------------------------

    /// Every object's world matrix, at the object's own slot. Read when `UseTransformRecords` is on.
    ///
    /// Per *frame* and not per view, because where an object is does not depend on who is looking —
    /// which is what lets the shadow pass and the camera pass read one buffer, and what would make a
    /// per-view copy of it so much waste.
    [PerFrame] var transforms: Buffer<ObjectTransform>

    /// The sun, kept separate from the list: it has no position to cull against, so it is in every
    /// cluster and paying list traversal for it would be paying for nothing.
    [PerFrame] var lightDirection: float3

    /// Which record this frame's objects start at, added to the instance index.
    ///
    /// **An index rather than a bound offset, and the reason is the host's.** The buffer holds one
    /// region per frame in flight — a descriptor bound at zero reads the region another frame is
    /// writing — and the route a resource takes to a set carries a handle a host named and has
    /// nowhere to put an offset. So the whole ring is bound and this says where this frame begins.
    /// `UploadBuffer` recommends the same thing for everything else it holds.
    ///
    /// Declared here rather than at the top of the block for the same reason `materialIndex` sits
    /// where it does: std140 leaves four bytes after a `float3`, so put here it costs the block
    /// nothing and put first it would cost sixteen.
    [PerFrame] var transformBase: int = 0

    [PerFrame] var lightColor: float3

    /// The scene's whole light list, which the clustered variant indexes into rather than copying.
    ///
    /// The same buffer `ClusterCulling` culled, bound to both passes. A cluster's entry is an *index*
    /// into it, so the light itself exists once however many clusters reach it — which is the reason
    /// the culler writes indices rather than lights.
    [PerFrame] var lightBuffer: Buffer<PunctualLight>

    /// One list per cluster, as `ClusterCulling` wrote it.
    ///
    /// Per *frame* rather than per view, and that is a real constraint rather than a shrug: the grid
    /// is built in one camera's view space, so a second camera drawing the same frame would read the
    /// first one's clusters. Set 1 cannot hold it — a buffer there would make two shaders' set 1
    /// incompatible and the shared view set unbindable — so a split-screen frame needs a cluster
    /// buffer per view and a set 0 per view to go with it. Written down because the shape that would
    /// hide it is the one that binds without complaining.
    [PerFrame] var clusters: Buffer<ClusterLights>

    /// The camera parameters that place a fragment in the grid. The same values the culling pass was
    /// given, because a fragment that computes its cluster differently reads someone else's lights.
    [PerFrame] var tanHalfFov: float2 = float2(1f, 0.5625f)

    [PerFrame] var nearPlane: float = 0.1f
    [PerFrame] var farPlane: float = 1000f

    /// How many cascades the shadow atlas holds.
    ///
    /// A permutation because it sizes an array *in the block*, so the block's size differs per
    /// variant — the same argument as `MaxLights`, and the reason neither is a `const`. A project
    /// shipping one cascade should not carry four matrices it never reads, and
    /// `ShadowMapRenderer.CascadeCount` has to match: the host fills as many slots as it fitted, and
    /// a shader reading past them projects with a matrix nobody wrote.
    [Permutation] val CascadeCount: int = 4

    /// The directional light's cascades, near first, each mapping into its own tile of the atlas.
    ///
    /// This was one matrix and one distance, which meant one cascade: everything past the nearest
    /// slice projected outside its tile and came back unshadowed. Four matrices and the distances
    /// they are valid to is what makes a cascaded atlas actually cascade, and the *selection* is
    /// per fragment because that is what a cascade is — a fragment's own distance deciding which
    /// resolution it is shadowed at.
    [PerFrame] var cascades: ShadowCascade[CascadeCount]

    /// Over what distance shadows fade out at the last cascade's end.
    ///
    /// Without it the shadow term stops at a line across the ground, which reads as a rendering
    /// error rather than as the end of the shadow distance. `Lighting.CascadeFade` is the ramp.
    [PerFrame] var shadowFadeRange: float = 10f

    [PerFrame] var shadowMap: Texture2D
    [PerFrame] var shadowSampler: Sampler
    [PerFrame] var shadowTexelSize: float2 = float2(0.0009765625f, 0.0009765625f)
    [PerFrame] var shadowConstantBias: float = 0.0005f
    [PerFrame] var shadowSlopeBias: float = 0.002f

    [PerFrame] var environment: TextureCube
    [PerFrame] var environmentSampler: Sampler
    [PerFrame] var environmentMipCount: float = 7f
    [PerFrame] var ambientIntensity: float = 1f

    /// Whether local reflection probes stand in for the environment where they apply.
    ///
    /// A permutation rather than a weight of zero, because the *work* differs: with it off there is
    /// no parallax correction, no second cube sample and no blend, which is most of the ambient term.
    ///
    /// ⚠ The cubes are still declared, and a host still binds them — see the note at the top of the
    /// file. A scene with no probes carries four cube descriptors it never samples, which is the cost
    /// this comment used to claim the permutation avoided.
    [Permutation] val UseReflectionProbe: bool = false

    /// How many probes a frame may have bound at once.
    ///
    /// A permutation because it sizes a *binding*: the array's length is in the descriptor set
    /// layout, so a project with four probes in view should not carry thirty-two descriptors. The
    /// same argument as `MaxLights`, one set further out.
    [Permutation] val ProbeCount: int = 4

    /// The probes' captures, prefiltered exactly as the environment is.
    ///
    /// One binding with a count rather than one binding per probe, which is what lets an object pick
    /// its own with an index instead of a descriptor set. The index comes from the per-draw block and
    /// is therefore uniform across a draw, so no descriptor-indexing extension is involved.
    [PerFrame] var probes: TextureCube[ProbeCount]

    [PerFrame] var probeSampler: Sampler

    /// Where each probe is and how it is corrected, in the same order as `probes`.
    [PerFrame] var probeVolumes: ReflectionProbeVolume[ProbeCount]

    /// The environment's diffuse irradiance, projected into nine coefficients per channel.
    ///
    /// Nine numbers rather than a texture, because irradiance is so smooth that order-2 spherical
    /// harmonics reproduce it to within a percent — and because the alternative this replaces was
    /// sampling the roughest mip of the reflection cube, which is a different integral: a mip is
    /// prefiltered against a GGX lobe and irradiance is the cosine lobe, so the ambient term was
    /// flatter than it should be everywhere and wrong at every silhouette.
    [PerFrame] var environmentSh: ShCoefficients

    stream var positionWS: float3
    stream var normalWS: float3
    stream var tangentWS: float4
    stream var uv: float2

    /// View-space position, for finding the fragment's cluster.
    ///
    /// Carried from the vertex stage rather than recovered in the fragment stage, because Raven has no
    /// fragment-stage position built-in — there is no `SV_Position` to read on the way in, only to
    /// write on the way out. Interpolating it costs a location, which the unclustered variant does
    /// not pay: the write below is inside the permutation, so with clustering off there is no write,
    /// and a stream nothing writes is not an output.
    stream var positionVS: float3

    /// The vertex stage.
    ///
    /// `SV_InstanceID` is taken whether or not the records are on, and taking it costs nothing: it is
    /// a built-in the stage is handed, not an input anybody has to bind. Reading it *conditionally*
    /// would be the wrong shape — a parameter cannot appear inside a permutation — and this way the
    /// two variants differ only in where the matrix comes from.
    [VertexShader]
    [Semantic("SV_Position")]
    func Vertex(position: float3, normal: float3, tangent: float4, texcoord: float2, [Semantic("SV_InstanceID")] instance: int): float4 {
        // Whichever way the host sends it. The permutation folds, so the variant that pushes has no
        // buffer read and the variant that reads has no push — rather than a branch every vertex takes
        // on a value that is the same for the whole frame.
        var objectToWorld = world

        if (UseTransformRecords) {
            objectToWorld = transforms[transformBase + instance].world
        }

        positionWS = Math.TransformPoint(objectToWorld, position)
        normalWS = Math.TransformNormalRigid(objectToWorld, normal)
        tangentWS = float4(Math.TransformDirection(objectToWorld, tangent.xyz), tangent.w)
        uv = texcoord

        if (UseClusteredLights) {
            positionVS = Math.TransformPoint(view, positionWS)
        }

        // Two matrices the stage already has, rather than their product uploaded per object.
        return Transform.ObjectToClip(viewProjection, positionWS)
    }

    [FragmentShader]
    [Semantic("SV_Target")]
    func Fragment(): float4 {
        // The material fills the surface. Every feature reads what the previous one left, which is
        // what `inout` buys: adding a feature to the chain changes no other feature's signature.
        var d: MaterialData
        MaterialDefaults.Begin(d, normalWS, tangentWS, uv)
        surface.Compute(d)

        val v = Math.SafeNormalize(viewPosition - positionWS)
        val n = Normals.FaceForward(Math.SafeNormalize(d.normalWS), v)
        val alpha = Brdf.Alpha(d.perceptualRoughness)

        var color = Direct(d, n, v, alpha)
        color += Punctual(d, n, v, alpha)

        if (UseImageBasedLighting) {
            color += Ambient(d, n, v)
        }

        return float4(color + d.emissive, d.alpha)
    }

    /// The directional light's contribution, shadowed.
    ///
    /// The BRDF is the shading model's and the cosine is inside it, so what is left here is the
    /// light: its radiance, and whether this fragment can see it.
    func Direct(d: MaterialData, n: float3, v: float3, alpha: float): float3 {
        val light = Lighting.Directional(lightDirection, lightColor)
        val angles = ShadingAngles.Of(n, v, light.l)

        var s: ShadingVectors
        s.n = n
        s.v = v
        s.l = light.l

        // Shadowed only where the surface faces the light. Not an early return any more, because a
        // scattering material's whole contribution lives past the terminator — but the shadow lookup
        // itself is still worth skipping, and a fragment facing away is in shadow by geometry.
        val visibility = (UseShadows && angles.NdotL > 0f) ? Shadow(n, angles.NdotL) : 1f

        return shading.Shade(d, angles, s, alpha) * light.radiance * visibility
    }

    /// Every light this fragment has to consider, unshadowed.
    ///
    /// Which list that is comes from the permutation, and only one of the two loops is emitted. The
    /// *body* is the same either way — `Contribution` — because the difference between clustered and
    /// unclustered shading is entirely about which lights you look at, and nothing about what you do
    /// with one. Duplicating the BRDF calls into both loops is how the two would come to disagree.
    func Punctual(d: MaterialData, n: float3, v: float3, alpha: float): float3 {
        if (UseClusteredLights) {
            return Clustered(d, n, v, alpha)
        }

        var total = float3(0f)

        // Bounded by `MaxLights` and broken out of at `lightCount`, rather than looped to
        // `lightCount` directly: the bound is what lets a driver unroll and what keeps the index
        // provably inside the array. The early exit costs nothing when the list is short.
        for (i in 0 .. MaxLights - 1) {
            if (i >= lightCount) {
                break
            }

            total += Contribution(lights[i], d, n, v, alpha)
        }

        return total
    }

    /// The lights the culling pass put in this fragment's cluster.
    ///
    /// Two indirections per light — cluster to index, index to light — and that is the trade the
    /// whole scheme makes: a dependent read per light against iterating lights that are nowhere near
    /// this fragment. It wins as soon as the scene has more lights than a cluster typically holds,
    /// which is the point at which anyone reaches for clustering in the first place.
    func Clustered(d: MaterialData, n: float3, v: float3, alpha: float): float3 {
        var total = float3(0f)

        val cluster = ClusterGrid.Of(positionVS, tanHalfFov, nearPlane, farPlane)

        if (cluster >= clusters.Length) {
            return total
        }

        val list = clusters[cluster]

        for (i in 0 .. ClusterGrid.Capacity - 1) {
            if (uint(i) >= list.count) {
                break
            }

            val index = int(list.indices[i])

            // The culler and this pass see the same buffer, so an index out of range means they were
            // given different ones — a host mistake that would otherwise read whatever follows the
            // buffer, which on a GPU is a wrong colour on one driver and a device loss on another.
            if (index < lightBuffer.Length) {
                total += Contribution(lightBuffer[index], d, n, v, alpha)
            }
        }

        return total
    }

    /// One light's contribution to this surface.
    ///
    /// The early-out asks the surface as well as the angle, and that is the price of scattering: a
    /// material with no scatter colour is answered by every model here with zero once the cosine is
    /// zero, so skipping it is free — and one that has one is brightest exactly there.
    func Contribution(light: PunctualLight, d: MaterialData, n: float3, v: float3, alpha: float): float3 {
        // The mirror direction, which is what a shaped light finds its representative point along. A
        // punctual light ignores it, and it is computed per light rather than once per fragment
        // because the light loop is where a shaded normal is already in registers — one `reflect` is
        // cheaper than the parameter it would otherwise have to be threaded through.
        val reflected = reflect(-v, n)

        val sample = Lighting.Resolve(light, positionWS, alpha, reflected)
        val angles = ShadingAngles.Of(n, v, sample.l)

        if (angles.NdotL <= 0f && Math.MaxComponent(d.scatterColor) <= 0f) {
            return float3(0f)
        }

        var s: ShadingVectors
        s.n = n
        s.v = v
        s.l = sample.l

        return shading.Shade(d, angles, s, sample.alphaPrime) * sample.radiance
    }

    /// Which cascade shades a fragment at this distance.
    ///
    /// The nearest one that still covers it, which is the whole idea: the cascades are fitted to
    /// successive slices of the camera's frustum, so the first whose `split` a fragment is inside is
    /// the highest-resolution map that contains it. Falling through to the last is what a fragment
    /// beyond the shadow distance gets, and `Lighting.CascadeFade` is what stops that being a line.
    ///
    /// Bounded by the permutation and broken out of, the same shape the light loop has: the bound is
    /// what lets a driver unroll it and what keeps the index provably inside the array.
    func CascadeOf(viewDepth: float): int {
        var index = CascadeCount - 1

        for (i in 0 .. CascadeCount - 1) {
            if (viewDepth <= cascades[i].split) {
                index = i
                break
            }
        }

        return index
    }

    /// The shadow term for this pixel, biased both ways — see `Lighting.ShadowBias`.
    ///
    /// The cascade is chosen from the fragment's own view depth rather than handed to the draw,
    /// which is what makes one atlas cover a whole frustum: a fragment two metres away and one two
    /// hundred metres away read different tiles of the same texture, at the resolution each needs.
    ///
    /// `view` is multiplied here rather than interpolated. A stream would cost every fragment in
    /// every variant a location, where this costs one matrix multiply in the shadowed one — and
    /// `positionVS` already exists for the clustered variant, which is the case that has a second
    /// use for it.
    func Shadow(n: float3, NdotL: float): float {
        val viewDepth = ClusterGrid.DepthOf(Math.TransformPoint(view, positionWS))
        val cascade = cascades[CascadeOf(viewDepth)]

        val offset = Lighting.NormalOffset(positionWS, n, NdotL, shadowTexelSize.x * 64f)
        val clip = cascade.viewProjection * float4(offset, 1f)
        val ndc = Math.ClipToNdc(clip)
        val shadowUv = Transform.NdcToUv(ndc)

        val bias = Lighting.ShadowBias(NdotL, shadowConstantBias, shadowSlopeBias)
        val shadow = Lighting.ShadowPcf3x3(shadowMap, shadowSampler, shadowUv, ndc.z - bias, shadowTexelSize)

        // Faded against the *last* cascade's end, which is where the shadow distance actually runs
        // out. Fading at every boundary would be the other thing this could mean, and it is not what
        // is wanted: a fragment crossing from one cascade to the next should keep its shadow, at a
        // different resolution.
        val fade = Lighting.CascadeFade(viewDepth, cascades[CascadeCount - 1].split, shadowFadeRange)
        return lerp(1f, shadow, fade)
    }

    /// Image-based ambient: a prefiltered environment for specular, spherical harmonics for diffuse.
    ///
    /// The split-sum approximation, with both halves now coming from where they should. **The mip is
    /// chosen by roughness**, which it was not: this sampled the reflection at level zero whatever the
    /// material said, so every surface mirrored the environment and `environmentMipCount` and
    /// `Ibl.SpecularLod` were both dead. A rough metal reflecting a sharp environment reads as a
    /// material problem rather than as a missing argument, which is why it survived.
    ///
    /// `SampleLevel` rather than `Sample`, and not only to state the level: a fragment shader's implicit
    /// derivatives are undefined under non-uniform flow, and this is called from inside one.
    func Ambient(d: MaterialData, n: float3, v: float3): float3 {
        val NdotV = saturate(dot(n, v))
        val reflected = reflect(-v, n)

        val dfg = Ibl.EnvironmentDfg(NdotV, d.perceptualRoughness)
        val lod = Ibl.SpecularLod(d.perceptualRoughness, environmentMipCount)

        var prefiltered = environment.SampleLevel(environmentSampler, reflected, lod).rgb

        if (UseReflectionProbe) {
            prefiltered = lerp(prefiltered, Probe(d.perceptualRoughness, reflected), saturate(probeWeight))
        }

        val irradiance = Ibl.IrradianceSh9(n, environmentSh)

        val specular = Ibl.Specular(prefiltered, d.f0, dfg, d.occlusion)
        val diffuse = Ibl.Diffuse(irradiance, d.diffuseColor, d.occlusion)
        return (specular + diffuse) * ambientIntensity
    }

    /// The local probe's radiance along a reflection, corrected for where it was captured from.
    ///
    /// **Without the correction a probe is worse than no probe.** A cubemap is captured from one
    /// point, so sampling it by direction alone reflects the room as seen from that point — a floor
    /// two metres away reflects the ceiling above the probe rather than what is above the floor, and
    /// the reflection slides as the camera moves. Re-aiming the direction from the probe's centre
    /// toward where the ray actually meets the probe's volume is what makes it sit still.
    func Probe(perceptualRoughness: float, reflected: float3): float3 {
        // Clamped rather than trusted: the index arrives in a per-object block a host filled, and
        // an out-of-range one reads a descriptor nobody wrote — a wrong reflection on one driver and
        // a device loss on another.
        val index = clamp(probeIndex, 0, ProbeCount - 1)
        val volume = probeVolumes[index]

        var corrected = Ibl.ParallaxCorrect(reflected, positionWS, volume.box)

        if (volume.radius > 0f) {
            corrected = Ibl.ParallaxCorrectSphere(reflected, positionWS, volume.box.center, volume.radius)
        }

        val lod = Ibl.SpecularLod(perceptualRoughness, volume.mipCount)
        return probes[index].SampleLevel(probeSampler, corrected, lod).rgb
    }
}
